Sure! Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^(adjective)\b/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  if (adjectives.length > 1) {
    const firstAdjective = adjectives.shift();
    adjectives.push(firstAdjective);
    const newSentence = adjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.filter(word => !word.match(/^(adjective)\b/)).join(" ");
    return newSentence;
  } else {
    return sentence;
  }
}
const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = switchAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```
This function splits the input sentence into an array of words, then filters out the adjectives and stores them in a separate array. If there are multiple adjectives, it swaps the first and last adjectives in the array and joins the array elements back into a sentence. If there are no adjectives, the function returns the input sentence unchanged.

